  ex:l8-minimum-two-coefficients-script-2 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 8 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-minimum-two-coefficients-formula ; dct:title "Squared error, total differential and minimum conditions"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-ch1 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 1 ; dct:title "From a time trend to a criterion"@en ; cd:body "**Linear Regression**\n\nToday we turn a time trend into a reproducible fitted model. We first agree what a good line means, then acquire enough matrix language to build a design matrix and derive the normal equations. The final applications change the known model terms without changing the linear coefficient problem. Uncertainty belongs to the next appointment.\n**A signal drifts during a run**\n\nDuring one run, we recorded the detector signal at the start and then once per minute. The figure pairs the detector setup with the five readings: 1.10, 2.80, 5.20, 6.90 and 9.00 mV at 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4 min, respectively. How can we predict the signal at 2.5 min, between two recorded times? A model describes signal as a function of time. We can evaluate it inside the measured interval (interpolation) or outside it (extrapolation). The dashed extension in the figure is an extrapolation beyond the last reading. Adding a model raises the next question: how do we choose a line that fits the measured points?\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-1-1]]\n\n**Estimate a model from measurements**\n\nTo describe the measured trend with a straight line, we estimate two numbers using all five time–signal pairs. The intercept gives the modelled signal at zero minutes in mV. The slope gives the modelled change in signal per minute in mV/min. The schematic shows how the measurements inform both coefficients. In the interactive plot, you can change each one and see what happens to the line.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-estimate-line]]\n\n**Fit by eye: two coefficients**\n\nTry different candidate lines for the same measurements. Change the intercept to raise or lower the line; change the slope to alter its tilt in mV per minute. The manual experiment starts at intercept 0 mV and slope 2.5 mV/min. As you move the sliders, compare the vertical gaps in the scatter plot with the residual bars. A point above the line appears above zero in the residual plot, and a point below the line appears below zero. Ask learners to improve the line and explain which vertical gaps their changes reduce. Do not reveal the fitted coefficient values yet. Compare the visible candidate curve with the fixed observation points; later the normal equations provide a reproducible solution.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-1-2]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-1-3]]\n\n**Give every vertical gap a sign**\n\nUse the adjustable candidate line in [[ref:ex:l8-script-material-1-2]] to connect the scatter plot with the residual bars. Compute each observation minus its prediction at exactly the same time. A point above the line has positive residual; one below has negative residual. Move the sliders and check how the signed residuals change. These are vertical signal gaps, not perpendicular geometric distances.\n\n**What happens when we add the signed gaps?**\n\nAdding the five residuals gives 0.00 mV. Each point still misses the fitted line: positive and negative gaps cancel. The next step is to define a score that counts gaps on both sides.\n**What should we minimise to fit a line?**\n\nThe comparison figure uses the same five measurements for two candidate lines. The starting line has intercept 0 mV and slope 2.5 mV/min, with residuals +1.10, +0.30, +0.20, −0.60 and −1.00 mV. The other line has intercept 1 mV and slope 2 mV/min, with residuals +0.10, −0.20, +0.20, −0.10 and 0.00 mV. Both signed sums are zero. Squaring the residuals before adding gives 2.70 mV² for the first line and 0.10 mV² for the second. A gap twice as large contributes four times as much. Absolute deviations would also prevent cancellation. The squared score is differentiable in the coefficients, which is useful when we derive the normal equations.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-1-7]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-1-8]]\n\n**Where is the squared error smallest?**\n\nWith the observations fixed, try changing one coefficient at a time. Each local error plot shows a slice of the squared-error surface near the corresponding slider setting. The other coefficient is held at its current value. Both plots mark the same current squared error. The total differential gives the first-order change in squared error for a change in both coefficients. At the joint minimum it is zero for every direction. We can vary one coefficient while keeping the other fixed. Doing this for each coefficient shows that both partial derivatives must be zero at the same pair of values. For these data, the quadratic curves upward and the pair is unique. To find the pair we need to solve the two equations together. Matrix notation gives us a compact way to do that.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-minimum-two-coefficients-script-1]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-minimum-two-coefficients-script-2]]"@en ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l8-script-material-1-1, ex:l8-script-material-estimate-line, ex:l8-script-material-1-2, ex:l8-script-material-1-3, ex:l8-script-material-1-7, ex:l8-script-material-1-8, ex:l8-minimum-two-coefficients-script-1, ex:l8-minimum-two-coefficients-script-2 ; dct:references ex:l8-script-material-1-1, ex:l8-script-material-estimate-line, ex:l8-script-material-1-2, ex:l8-script-material-1-3, ex:l8-script-material-1-7, ex:l8-script-material-1-8, ex:l8-minimum-two-coefficients-script-1, ex:l8-minimum-two-coefficients-script-2 ; cd:relatedScene ex:l8-scene-01, ex:l8-scene-02, ex:l8-scene-estimate-line-coefficients, ex:l8-scene-04, ex:l8-scene-07, ex:l8-minimum-two-coefficients-scene, ex:l8-squared-error-conditions-scene ; cd:authoredResource true .
