  ex:l8-script-material-2-23 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 6 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-normal-from-residuals-section ; dct:title "Least Squares: solve the normal equation"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-ch2 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 2 ; dct:title "Matrix algebra and simultaneous equations"@en ; cd:body "**Matrix Algebra**\n\nThis short detour introduces the operations we will use to express the two regression conditions together.\n\n**Matrix Shapes: count rows before columns**\n\nCompare the 2 by 3, 3 by 2 and 2 by 4 arrays. Count horizontal rows first and vertical columns second. The first two arrays contain the same number of entries, but their shapes differ.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-2]]\n\n**Matrix Indexing: row first, then column**\n\nRead a subscript pair in a fixed order. In the shown matrix, a subscript 23 means row 2 then column 3, so its value is 6. It does not mean row 3 column 2, and it does not mean the number twenty-three. The box marks that matrix entry.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-4]]\n\n**Matrix Types: square matrices**\n\nCompare the 2 by 2, 3 by 3 and 4 by 4 examples. Every one is square because its row and column counts match. The positions and values of the entries can vary.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-6]]\n\n**Matrix Types: diagonal matrices**\n\nCompare diagonal matrices of sizes 2, 3 and 4. The highlighted diagonal is where the row and column indices agree. Every off-diagonal entry is zero. The 3 by 3 example has a zero on its main diagonal, which makes it singular.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-7]]\n\n**Matrix Types: identity matrices**\n\nCompare the identities of sizes 1, 2 and 3. Every diagonal entry is one. The left identity for a 2 by 3 matrix must have size 2, and the right identity must have size 3. Both products preserve the same matrix A.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-8]]\n\n**Matrix Arithmetic: addition and subtraction**\n\nBoth matrices have the same shape. Read the top-left cells first: add 1 and 5 or subtract 5 from 1. Then do the same at each of the other three positions. The result keeps the original shape. When entries have units, the two values being combined must have compatible units.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-9]]\n\n**Matrix Arithmetic: multiplication by a scalar**\n\nMultiply each entry by the same scalar. The first calculation multiplies 1 by 2, and the remaining cells repeat the same operation with 2, 3 and 4. The positions and the shape stay the same. A dimensionless scalar preserves the units of the entries.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-10]]\n\n**Matrix Transpose: rows become columns**\n\nThe first row [1,2,3] becomes the first column and the second row [4,5,6] becomes the second column. Shape two-by-three therefore becomes three-by-two. Transposing twice returns the original matrix. Later X transpose will collect dot products with the columns of X.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-11]]\n\n**Matrix Multiplication: which dimensions must match?**\n\nA matrix with m rows and n columns can multiply one with n rows and p columns. Each output entry needs a complete row and a complete column of equal length. The result has m rows and p columns. As a quick question, a two-by-three times a three-by-four is valid and yields two-by-four; the reverse order is not valid. Pause for the shape answer before continuing.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-12]]\n\n**Matrix Multiplication: one row meets one column**\n\nEach row of A has three entries and each column of B has three entries. Start with the boxed row and column. Multiply the three matching pairs and add to get 5 in the top-left cell. The other three cells use the three remaining row-column pairs and give 13, 14 and 31. The result has two rows from A and two columns from B.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-14]]\n\n**Matrix Multiplication: sum the squares of a column**\n\nTranspose the n by 1 column so that it becomes one row. Multiplying that row by the original column pairs each entry with itself and adds the products. For entries 2, 3 and 4 the result is 4 plus 9 plus 16, which is 29.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-column-squares-script]]\n\n**Matrix Multiplication: why order matters**\n\nUse a new pair of two-by-two matrices so both products have the same dimensions. The order still matters: AB and BA have different entries. Noncommutativity is about the operation, not a rounding error. For rectangular matrices one order may not even exist. Do not rearrange matrix factors as though they were scalar numbers.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-16]]\n\n**Matrix Transpose: three useful rules**\n\nSubtraction is entrywise, so swapping rows and columns preserves the difference between matching entries. For a product, the row of A and column of B become a column of A transpose and a row of B transpose. Their dot product is preserved by reversing the factors. Transposing a second time swaps the rows and columns back.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-transpose-rules-script]]\n\n**Matrix Arithmetic: entrywise multiplication**\n\nHadamard multiplication combines corresponding entries of equally shaped matrices. For the same A and B it gives rows [5,12] and [21,32]. This is neither AB nor BA. In R, an asterisk is entrywise multiplication and percent-star-percent is matrix multiplication. Keep that distinction when reading model code.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-17]]\n\n**Matrix Inverse: undoing a matrix product**\n\nFor the new example A = [[2,1],[1,1]], the inverse is [[1,−1],[−1,2]]. Check either multiplication order: the diagonal products sum to one and off-diagonal products sum to zero. An inverse is not obtained by taking reciprocals of individual entries. The inverse exists only for nonsingular square matrices.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-19]]\n\n**Matrix Equations: write two equations as one**\n\nThe equations 2u1 + u2 = 5 and u1 + u2 = 3 become Au = b with the same inverse example and b = [5,3]. Left-multiply by the inverse to obtain u = [2,1]. Substitute back: 2 times 2 plus 1 equals 5 and 2 plus 1 equals 3. Row equations and the matrix equation describe the same problem.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-2-22]]\n\n**Evaluate a line at five points**\n\nUse the known intercept 2 and slope 0.5. Read the times in the order shown on the slide. Work through the first input 10, then build the whole matrix. The first column of ones carries the intercept into every row and the second column contains the five inputs. Each row times the same coefficient column yields one calculated response. Finally name the three objects X, b and y.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-five-point-matrix-script]]"@en ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l8-script-material-2-2, ex:l8-script-material-2-4, ex:l8-script-material-2-6, ex:l8-script-material-2-7, ex:l8-script-material-2-8, ex:l8-script-material-2-9, ex:l8-script-material-2-10, ex:l8-script-material-2-11, ex:l8-script-material-2-12, ex:l8-script-material-2-14, ex:l8-column-squares-script, ex:l8-script-material-2-16, ex:l8-transpose-rules-script, ex:l8-script-material-2-17, ex:l8-script-material-2-19, ex:l8-script-material-2-22, ex:l8-five-point-matrix-script ; dct:references ex:l8-script-material-2-2, ex:l8-script-material-2-4, ex:l8-script-material-2-6, ex:l8-script-material-2-7, ex:l8-script-material-2-8, ex:l8-script-material-2-9, ex:l8-script-material-2-10, ex:l8-script-material-2-11, ex:l8-script-material-2-12, ex:l8-script-material-2-14, ex:l8-column-squares-script, ex:l8-script-material-2-16, ex:l8-transpose-rules-script, ex:l8-script-material-2-17, ex:l8-script-material-2-19, ex:l8-script-material-2-22, ex:l8-five-point-matrix-script ; cd:relatedScene ex:l8-scene-08, ex:l8-scene-09, ex:l8-scene-10, ex:l8-scene-12, ex:l8-scene-13, ex:l8-scene-14, ex:l8-scene-15, ex:l8-scene-16, ex:l8-scene-17, ex:l8-scene-18, ex:l8-scene-19, ex:l8-column-squares-scene, ex:l8-scene-20, ex:l8-transpose-rules-scene, ex:l8-scene-21, ex:l8-scene-22, ex:l8-scene-24, ex:l8-five-point-matrix-scene ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-3-1 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-media-design-build ; dct:title "Design Matrix: build it from the measurements – figure"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-3-2 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-row ; dct:title "Design Matrix: one row per observation – formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-3-3 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 3 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-design ; dct:title "Design Matrix: one column per model term – formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-3-4 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 4 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-prediction ; dct:title "Matrix Prediction: multiply by the coefficient column – formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-3-5 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 5 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-units ; dct:title "Matrix Prediction: check the units – formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-4-2 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 1 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-residual-column-formula ; dct:title "Least Squares: put the residuals into a column"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-4-5 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 2 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-matrix-squared-error-section ; dct:title "Least Squares: write squared error as a matrix product"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-4-6 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 4 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-matrix-differential-section ; dct:title "Least Squares: differentiate term by term"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-4-8 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 21 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-direct-numbers ; dct:title "The numerical coefficient matrix and right-hand side"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-4-9 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 22 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-direct-inverse ; dct:title "Invert the numerical coefficient matrix"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-4-11 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 23 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-direct-fitted ; dct:title "Compute the numerical intercept and slope"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-4-12 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 24 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-fitted ; dct:title "Return to the observations: formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-4-column-figure a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 7 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-media-dependent-columns ; dct:title "Dependent columns and identical predictions"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-4-13 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 8 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-f-rank ; dct:title "Why duplicate columns fail: formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-material-4-14 a cd:ScriptMaterial ; cd:position 9 ; cd:scriptResource ex:l8-r-code ; dct:title "Read a small R fit without running R: formula and complete symbol legend"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l8-script-ch4 a cd:ScriptSection ; cd:position 4 ; dct:title "Design matrix and fitted responses"@en ; cd:body "**Design Matrix: build it from the measurements**\n\nThe design matrix records what we assume the response can depend on. Rows keep the observation order; columns record known model terms. These columns encode the model terms used to fit the coefficients. Start with the constant-plus-time model, retaining exactly the same drift dataset.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-3-1]]\n\n**Design Matrix: one row per observation**\n\nAt the third observation the time is 2 min and the measured signal is 5.20 mV. Its design row is [1,2]: one for the intercept and the observed time for the slope term. The signal is not inserted into X; it remains the matching entry of y. Stage through the design build to keep each response aligned with its own row.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-3-1]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-3-2]]\n\n**Design Matrix: one column per model term**\n\nThe first design column consists of five ones, allowing a common intercept. The second contains 0,1,2,3,4 min. Coefficients must be ordered intercept then slope. Swapping columns is permissible only if the coefficient order changes with them. The design definition is graph-owned and separate from its illustrated construction.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-3-1]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-3-3]]\n\n**Matrix Prediction: multiply by the coefficient column**\n\nA five-by-two X multiplies a two-by-one coefficient column to give a five-by-one prediction column. The third row computes intercept plus 2 min times slope. Repeat for all rows without changing the coefficients between observations. This is the same row-dot-column multiplication as the small numerical example.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-3-1]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-3-4]]\n\n**Matrix Prediction: check the units**\n\nThe constant column is dimensionless; the time column has unit min. Therefore the intercept coefficient needs mV and the slope coefficient mV/min. With the fitted values, the third prediction is 1.02 mV plus 2 min times 1.99 mV/min, giving 5.00 mV. A coefficient column is an ordered collection and its entries need not have identical units.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-3-5]]\n\n\n\nTranspose the design matrix so that its constant column becomes a row of five ones and its time column becomes a row of the five observed times. Multiply this two-by-five array by the five-by-one fitted residual column. Row 1 adds the residuals with dimensionless weights, giving mV; row 2 adds time-weighted residuals, giving min·mV. These are exactly the two conditions established earlier at the least-squares minimum, now collected in one two-entry column. For the fitted residuals 0.08, -0.21, 0.20, -0.09 and 0.02 mV at times 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4 min, both totals are zero. Multiplication itself works for any column, but arbitrary errors do not generally give zero. Each design column gives one residual condition. The following material uses the same native derivation as the scene, including its input, operation, result and authored legends.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-residual-orthogonality]]\n\n**Why duplicate columns fail**\n\nFor p coefficient columns, full column rank means rank(X) = p. If a second constant column is appended, increasing one constant coefficient and decreasing the other by the same amount leaves every prediction unchanged. X transpose X is then singular and coefficient values are not unique. More rows do not repair duplicate columns. For the drift design, the time column is not a multiple of the constant column, so both coefficients are identified.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-4-column-figure]]\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-4-13]]\n\n**Read a small R fit without running R**\n\nThe authored code reads: x <- 0:4; y <- c(1.10,2.80,5.20,6.90,9.00); fit <- lm(y ~ x); coef(fit); fitted(fit); residuals(fit); sum(residuals(fit)^2). The tilde describes response by predictor, and lm includes the intercept by default. Expected coefficients are (Intercept) 1.02 and x 1.99; expected SSE is 0.099. model.matrix(fit) shows the columns of ones and x. The function uses a numerically preferable QR-based fit, not our explicit inverse. No external R runtime is needed to read these results; do not introduce summary(fit) inference in this appointment.\n\n[[ref:ex:l8-script-material-4-14]]"@en ; cd:hasScriptMaterial ex:l8-script-material-3-1, ex:l8-script-material-3-2, ex:l8-script-material-3-3, ex:l8-script-material-3-4, ex:l8-script-material-3-5, ex:l8-script-material-4-column-figure, ex:l8-script-material-4-13, ex:l8-script-material-4-14 ; dct:references ex:l8-script-material-3-1, ex:l8-script-material-3-2, ex:l8-script-material-3-3, ex:l8-script-material-3-4, ex:l8-script-material-3-5, ex:l8-script-material-4-column-figure, ex:l8-script-material-4-13, ex:l8-script-material-4-14 ; cd:relatedScene ex:l8-scene-26, ex:l8-scene-27, ex:l8-scene-28, ex:l8-scene-29, ex:l8-scene-30, ex:l8-scene-41, ex:l8-scene-42 ; cd:authoredResource true .
